{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/813262-high-mantle-and-clarity-protection","id":813262,"title":"High Mantle and Clarity protection","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-01-21T02:38:18-08:00","id":813262,"title":"High Mantle and Clarity protection","content":"In Changeling 1E, when a Changeling's wyrd reached 7 or higher it became more difficult for the lost to hold onto their clarity. However, Arcanearts has mentioned a few times on this forum that changelings with a high court mantle have a degree of protection from the True Fae in 2E. This had me wondering, is there a possibility of court mantles of 6 or higher acting as a code of morality that shield's the changeling's clarity to a certain degree?<br /> <br /> This would make a certain degree of sense to me, because changeling's join courts as a form of protection against the True Fae and to anchor themselves to the mundane world.<br /> <br /> <br /> This could be fun if the higher mantle reprograms the changelings personality. For example, giving high mantle changelings of the Autumn court personalities that are creepy and kooky the way the Addams family and denizens of Halloween Town (<b><i>The Nightmare Before Christmas</i></b>). Your a very nice guy, it's just that your idea of a great child's present is a blood drinking vampire teddy bear and there's nothing more romantic to you then waltzing through a haunted grave yard.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11066-madhatter","name":"Madhatter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11066_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"746"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-21T11:41:50-08:00","id":813520,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Madhatter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/813262-high-mantle-and-clarity-protection#post813262\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">However, Arcanearts has mentioned a few times on this forum that changelings with a high court mantle have a degree of protection from the True Fae in 2E.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm reasonably certain that's to do with high Mantle giving you a Personal Approach and the Fae having to abide by every Approach they encounter, though Arc can of course come and clarify himself within the bounds of stuff he's allowed to talk about.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-21T14:12:16-08:00","id":813642,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Madhatter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/813262-high-mantle-and-clarity-protection#post813262\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> In Changeling 1E, when a Changeling's wyrd reached 7 or higher it became more difficult for the lost to hold onto their clarity. However, Arcanearts has mentioned a few times on this forum that changelings with a high court mantle have a degree of protection from the True Fae in 2E. This had me wondering, is there a possibility of court mantles of 6 or higher acting as a code of morality that shield's the changeling's clarity to a certain degree?<br /> <br /> This would make a certain degree of sense to me, because changeling's join courts as a form of protection against the True Fae and to anchor themselves to the mundane world.<br /> <br /> <br /> This could be fun if the higher mantle reprograms the changelings personality. For example, giving high mantle changelings of the Autumn court personalities that are creepy and kooky the way the Addams family and denizens of Halloween Town (<b><i>The Nightmare Before Christmas</i></b>). Your a very nice guy, it's just that your idea of a great child's present is a blood drinking vampire teddy bear and there's nothing more romantic to you then waltzing through a haunted grave yard.</div> </div> </div> </div>I remember in 1e there was an Entitlement to the effect that you become an immortal vizier, but are not allowed to lead, only advise. And joining that order gives you protection from going insane. I wonder if something to that effect will return or not. <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9830-reshyshira","name":"ReshyShira","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9830_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"589"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-21T14:38:59-08:00","id":813664,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Madhatter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/813262-high-mantle-and-clarity-protection#post813262\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> In Changeling 1E, when a Changeling's wyrd reached 7 or higher it became more difficult for the lost to hold onto their clarity. However, Arcanearts has mentioned a few times on this forum that changelings with a high court mantle have a degree of protection from the True Fae in 2E. This had me wondering, is there a possibility of court mantles of 6 or higher acting as a code of morality that shield's the changeling's clarity to a certain degree?<br /> <br /> This would make a certain degree of sense to me, because changeling's join courts as a form of protection against the True Fae and to anchor themselves to the mundane world.<br /> <br /> <br /> This could be fun if the higher mantle reprograms the changelings personality. For example, giving high mantle changelings of the Autumn court personalities that are creepy and kooky the way the Addams family and denizens of Halloween Town (<b><i>The Nightmare Before Christmas</i></b>). Your a very nice guy, it's just that your idea of a great child's present is a blood drinking vampire teddy bear and there's nothing more romantic to you then waltzing through a haunted grave yard.</div> </div> </div> </div>Did I say that? I mean, I wasn't wrong, but I don't think I ever put it that way.<br /> <br /> So, here's what's going on. A high Mantle changeling has a personal approach that has to be appeased before a Huntsman can pick up Yearning. For the True Fae, that's another <i>law</i> they have to obey when dealing with that changeling.<br /> <br /> Furthermore, Approaches as a general rule are bans that a True Fae has to fulfill before they can do anything else to a changeling, so any courtier is protected.<br /> <br /> A changeling's mantle <i>can</i> help them with their Clarity, but it has to be a purposeful entry on the merit. It was one of my intentions for the Blue Rose Court's Mantle for them to get a rote effect on Clarity rolls before some changes were made to the freehold system. But by default, Mantles don't do a thing for Clarity.<br /> <br /> Oh, and the eldritch order you're thinking of Reshy is the Office of Vizerial Council. It's one of my favorites, and you can expect to see a Second Edition version as soon as we figure out how we're going to present Entitlements post-core.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 01-21-2016, 06:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-21T14:51:41-08:00","id":813672,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/813262-high-mantle-and-clarity-protection?p=813664#post813664\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh, and the eldritch order you're thinking of Reshy is the Office of Vizerial Council. It's one of my favorites, and you can expect to see a Second Edition version as soon as we figure out how we're going to present Entitlements post-core.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, it's probably my favorite entitlement from 1e. Liked the whole aspect(?) of an old changeling that's long since been divorced from decision making processes and yet still choosing to serve for the betterment(?) of their kind.<br /> <br /> I do want to ask though, because this was never really explained in 1e from what I saw; but how does a Vizier change who they're advising? It says you can never betray your king, but says you can change which you follow. So some things I always got hung up on was, say as an adviser your king decides to sell out you or your freehold to the Gentry. How would it work in this case? Do you stop being a Vizier because you refuse to be traded to the Gentry for a pile of Tokens of Respite? What if the king is making a back-door deal with the Gentry to get all of the Freehold dragged back to the Faerie, if you intervene does this make you stop being a Vizier? If the King says that choosing to serve another king would be Betrayal, would that make you stop being a Vizier if you wish to serve another? <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9830-reshyshira","name":"ReshyShira","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9830_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"589"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-21T15:15:03-08:00","id":813682,"title":"","content":"A Vizier is bound to the freehold first, serving the current ruler(Or rulers, as might be the case, such as the Sovereigns of the Married). In a Seasonal Freehold, they shift their service with the seasons, in Paris, they kind of permanently sit on the council, though they only have to offer advice to the current High Sovereigns, and in a Scheherazade freehold, they serve the current Sultana. if a Vizier wants to change their service entirely, they need to make arrangements with another freehold and establish that connection with the blessing of their rulers with the Wyrd.<br /> <br /> A Vizier caught with a dubious set of rulers is going to be in for some trouble. We'll go backwards on this.<br /> <br /> 1) A Ruler can't stop a Vizier from serving other rulers, because of the Vizier's ties to the freehold rather than any one individual. If the Vizier wanted to serve in a different freehold, though, the Vizier would be fine until they accepted a Pledge to serve the other one, at which point the mantle of the eldritch order would break. As an example, Paris has the Rose Courts and a small Scheherazade freehold. If the Rose Vizier accepted Jabir al-Abbas's offer to serve under the Sultana, they would fall out and rapidly age then and psycho-spiritually break.<br /> <br /> 2) A Vizier trying to stop a Ruler from selling out their freehold or themselves is fine so long as they don't bring harm to the ruler in question, So they can say &quot;Fuck off&quot; to them if they tried to bargain with their lives or such. But It's not hard for those situations to escalate either.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-21T15:25:53-08:00","id":813692,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/813262-high-mantle-and-clarity-protection?p=813682#post813682\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A Vizier is bound to the freehold first, serving the current ruler(Or rulers, as might be the case, such as the Sovereigns of the Married). In a Seasonal Freehold, they shift their service with the seasons, in Paris, they kind of permanently sit on the council, though they only have to offer advice to the current High Sovereigns, and in a Scheherazade freehold, they serve the current Sultana. if a Vizier wants to change their service entirely, they need to make arrangements with another freehold and establish that connection with the blessing of their rulers with the Wyrd.<br /> <br /> A Vizier caught with a dubious set of rulers is going to be in for some trouble. We'll go backwards on this.<br /> <br /> 1) A Ruler can't stop a Vizier from serving other rulers, because of the Vizier's ties to the freehold rather than any one individual. If the Vizier wanted to serve in a different freehold, though, the Vizier would be fine until they accepted a Pledge to serve the other one, at which point the mantle of the eldritch order would break. As an example, Paris has the Rose Courts and a small Scheherazade freehold. If the Rose Vizier accepted Jabir al-Abbas's offer to serve under the Sultana, they would fall out and rapidly age then and psycho-spiritually break.<br /> <br /> 2) A Vizier trying to stop a Ruler from selling out their freehold or themselves is fine so long as they don't bring harm to the ruler in question, So they can say &quot;Fuck off&quot; to them if they tried to bargain with their lives or such. But It's not hard for those situations to escalate either.</div> </div> </div> </div>So a Vizier cannot change which freehold it serves? What if the freehold is destroyed or disbanded?<br /> <br /> Also, unrelated question but let's say the king and (maybe) the Council get killed, captured, or overthrown somehow and are rendered unable to govern. Can a Vizier act as a sort of interim form of leadership or governance until a new leadership can be established? <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9830-reshyshira","name":"ReshyShira","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9830_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"589"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-21T15:30:37-08:00","id":813694,"title":"","content":"They can, actually. At the time of writing, I didn't have the &quot;harm&quot; clause in mind, so a Vizier can swap freeholds so long as the freehold they go to accepts them as the Vizier. My bad.<br /> <br /> And yeah, that seems reasonable, though the Vizier can't take actions to keep Rulers from stepping up.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-21T15:33:27-08:00","id":813699,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/813262-high-mantle-and-clarity-protection?p=813694#post813694\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They can, actually. At the time of writing, I didn't have the &amp;quot;harm&amp;quot; clause in mind, so a Vizier can swap freeholds so long as the freehold they go to accepts them as the Vizier. My bad.<br /> <br /> And yeah, that seems reasonable, though the Vizier can't take actions to keep Rulers from stepping up.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, but I was wondering if the need to not act as leadership outweighed the needs of the freehold in desperate times. In times of doubt or strife, the members of the freehold would likely look to a constant for support. In that case, the constant is the Vizier.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Also, the reason I kind of like the Viziers is that in a way they're like the Superego of the Freehold, the conscious that tries to get you to do the right thing but ultimately has no control over the decision making process. This is paired with the theme that he Vizier is likely the strongest Changeling there, and yet, despite this the Vizier works to enact change through words rather than displays of power. Because, ultimately, the freehold must be able to make mistakes, as any attempt to remove the choice from them will ultimately breed resentment. It's a freewill thing, I suppose you could say.<br /> <br /> At least, that's how I see it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":9830,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9830-reshyshira","user":"ReshyShira","date":". 01-21-2016, 07:19 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9830-reshyshira","name":"ReshyShira","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9830_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"589"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-22T12:21:38-08:00","id":814367,"title":"","content":"I think when the main Seasonal and Seelie Courts folded and combined to form the Silver Court in Toronto it would only be natural for any Viziers from the Seelie and Seasonal Courts to offer they're sevices to the Sultana. Or move.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-22T14:31:43-08:00","id":814457,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/813262-high-mantle-and-clarity-protection?p=814367#post814367\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think when the main Seasonal and Seelie Courts folded and combined to form the Silver Court in Toronto it would only be natural for any Viziers from the Seelie and Seasonal Courts to offer they're sevices to the Sultana. Or move.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, I do think they should have the ability to move on. First and foremost they should serve changelings as a whole, freeholds as a second, and the ruler third.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9830-reshyshira","name":"ReshyShira","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9830_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"589"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}